            HUMAN_INPUT_PROMPT=$(python3 "$QUEUE_HELPER" --dir "$LOG_DIR" prompt "$VERIFIED_INPUT_KEY") \
                || fail "could not load frozen human input."
        fi
        ;;
esac
INPUT_CONTEXT_PROMPT="Verified frozen input context for this invocation:
Requests in immutable cycle snapshot: $INPUT_COUNT
Allowed INPUT_STATUS: $EXPECTED_INPUT_STATUS
The queue prompt identifies which requests still need incorporation. Already incorporated or satisfied requests are not fresh input. An empty batch is NOT an active instruction. Repository goals, unresolved implementation work and historical logs do not count as active queued instructions. Use only the allowed status values above."

# Controller-owned step identity; history is imported once from executed headings.
STEP_INDEX_PROMPT=""
case "$MODE" in
    --review-only|--plan-only)
        WORK_CONTEXT=$(progress context "${AUTOCYCLE_INPUT_BATCH:-}") || fail "cannot load work registry."
        STEP_INDEX_PROMPT="
Step indexing:
AUTOCYCLE_WORK_CONTEXT: $WORK_CONTEXT
- The controller registry is authoritative. Mentions, suggestions and reservations never consume a step ID. Never reserve or certify a prospective next ID in RESULT.md.
- Keep the same ID while finishing the same bounded attempt, including process cycles, interruptions, resume and extension. A distinct bounded continuation requires opening Review of the previous attempt.
- Only Plan revises IMPLEMENTATION.md. Every new major Step has one concise ## Completion statement defining the state that finishes it. Preserve that parent Completion verbatim through every continuation. Never weaken it because implementation is difficult. Ordinary tasks remain checklist items.
- New work uses Step <session>.<step>[.<sub-step>], such as Step 2.14. Start a major at Step 2.14, never .0 or .1. After Review finds Completion unsatisfied and chooses to keep pursuing it, Plan may select a distinct bounded continuation with minor: 1 in AUTOCYCLE_PLAN, then minor: 2, etc. Omit minor when simply finishing the current attempt. Repairs, changed approaches or additional testing can be distinct continuations when warranted; never increment just for another cycle. No fourth level. COMPLETE closes the entire major sequence; the next genuinely distinct major is Step 2.15. Do not rewrite historical RESULT records or commits.
- Numbering and wording discrepancies alone are advisory. Do not open another numbering repair unless the discrepancy prevents execution or makes acceptance materially ambiguous; explain the concrete consequence.
- Do not reconstruct consumed IDs from prose or reserved numbers. Historical registry entries mark execution, not verified acceptance.
"
        ;;
esac

# Shared project-history guidance for Review, Plan and Implement; no evidence archive.
IFS= read -r -d '' PROJECT_HISTORY <<'HISTORY_EOF' || true
Git-first project history:
- Git is authoritative for tracked historical project state. For every implementation resolve B from the recorded IMPLEMENT_BASE_SHA in .git/autocycle/resume-state when available; otherwise use the authenticated Plan/implementation baseline from normal ownership and recovery (implementation-baseline.json, the bound work attempt and latest-implementation). Authenticate branch, ancestry and implementation/checkpoint binding through those normal mechanisms. Do not select a migration-specific baseline or invent recovery. If no authenticated baseline can be established, fail closed.
- For migration-related Review evaluate in order: (1) authenticated B, (2) historical Git blobs at B, (3) current canonical destinations/files, (4) provenance/path continuity, (5) current build/check behavior, (6) relevant regressions and native verification, (7) other evidence explicitly required by active user instructions, SESSION.md or current IMPLEMENTATION.md. Use git show B:path or git cat-file to inspect/materialize prior content directly; do not substitute a transient working-tree observation for B.
- Tracked move: identify the intended canonical destination from the implementation diff/current architecture, compare its bytes with the old Git blob at B (exact identity, SHA-256 and byte size where useful), and verify normal provenance/path mapping. Matching content with provenance continuity is a preserved relocation. Allow the old path to disappear; no duplicate legacy copy, receipt, backup or human approval is required for migration evidence.
- Tracked deletion without an identical surviving destination: Git retains historical bytes. Historical existence needs no receipt or extra copy. Judge removal against current requirements: obsolete/generated/redundant material may be removed when architecture, build/check and regressions support removal; required source/data/functionality may not be lost. An absent old path alone is not a blocker.
- Tracked replacement/modification: compare the Git version at B with the current filesystem only as current acceptance requires. Legitimately changed generated output does not require byte identity; use semantic, build/check, regression and provenance verification. Byte mismatch blocks a claimed identical relocation, not every intentional replacement.
- Non-Git generated, temporary, cache or redundant state has no historical preservation requirement by default. Current correctness and active instructions govern removal; do not create receipts, backups or approval prompts solely for removal.
- Non-Git irreplaceable source evidence: before the old copy disappears, establish the intended canonical destination and verify content continuity while both copies are available, preserving normal provenance. Without a surviving destination, block the destructive operation. No migration backup or human approval is required.
- Explicit exact-state requirements in active user instructions, SESSION.md or current IMPLEMENTATION.md remain binding, including exact non-Git artifacts. Do not create automatic waivers, backups or receipts for this exceptional case. Missing canonical source/data, relocation content mismatch, broken provenance, actual loss of required source evidence, failed build/check/regression or native verification, and unmet active requirements remain defects.
- Fresh Review must re-evaluate an old migration blocker from B and the active acceptance contract. Discard a historical exact-artifact requirement when the requested artifact is not represented by B, is not reconstructible from Git, and is not explicitly required by active user instructions, SESSION.md or current IMPLEMENTATION.md. This is a stale requirement based on the wrong historical reference point, not evidence satisfying acceptance. Old Review observations and delivered instructions do not create permanent acceptance requirements or get replayed as new authority. Do not require an obsolete path, legacy tree or non-baseline transient artifact merely because a previous Review referenced it. Never claim later verification proves an earlier gate ran.
- Preserve normal baseline, ownership attribution, provider retry, human adoption, interruption, candidate validation, protected-document, unrelated-dirty-work and Review-cache safeguards. Git preserves project history; .git/autocycle preserves controller/recovery evidence Git does not provide.
HISTORY_EOF
export AUTOCYCLE_PROJECT_HISTORY="$PROJECT_HISTORY"

